    "https://osu.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/OSUCareers/job/College-of-MedicineOffice-of-Health-Sciences/Associate-Dean-for-Graduate-Medical-Education--Designated-Institutional-Official--and-Associate-Medical-Director-for-GME_R161148-1": {
      "title": "Associate Dean for Graduate Medical Education, Designated Institutional Official, and Associate Medical Director for GME",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-16\nFull time\n\nScreen reader users may encounter difficulty with this site. For assistance with applying, please contact hr-accessibleapplication@osu.edu . If you have questions while submitting an application, please review these frequently asked questions . Current Employees and Students: If you are currently employed or enrolled as a student at The Ohio State University, please l og in to Workday to use the internal application process. Welcome to The Ohio State University's career site.  We invite you to apply to positions of interest. In order to ensure your application is complete, you must complete the following: Ensure you have all necessary documents available when starting the application process. You can review the additional job description section on postings for documents that may be required. You will be presented with the opportunity to attach up to five (5) documents in the Application Documents section. If necessary, please consider combining documents into one file. If you require any assistance with including the necessary documents in the application process, please reach out to HRConnection@osu.edu . Resume parsing is not effective on documents which exceed one (1) page.  As a result, it is not recommended to select Autofill with Resume when applying if using a resume or CV which exceeds one (1) page. Prior to submitting your application, please review and update (if necessary) the information in your candidate profile as it will transfer to your application.  Job Title: Associate Dean for Graduate Medical Education, Designated Institutional Official, and Associate Medical Director for GME Department: Opportunity Overview The Ohio State University College of Medicine and Wexner Medical Center seek an exceptional Associate Dean for Graduate Medical Education (GME), Designated Institutional Official (DIO) and Associate Medical Director for Graduate Medical Education. This senior leadership position provides strategic oversight, administration, quality management, operational leadership, and regulatory compliance for all graduate medical education programs sponsored by The Ohio State University Wexner Medical Center. The Associate Dean for GME serves as the Designated Institutional Official (DIO), institutional representative to accrediting and regulatory bodies, and Associate Medical Director for University Graduate Medical Education. The role ensures a high-quality clinical learning environment that advances resident and fellow education, patient safety, physician well-being, academic excellence, institutional accreditation, and alignment with organizational goals. The role operates within a dual reporting structure, reporting directly to the Vice Dean for Education and the Chief Clinical Officer.   The GME office serves as the liaison between accrediting agencies and The Ohio State University Wexner Medical Center GME training programs. The GME office oversees and monitors the activities of our residency and fellowship programs and provides resources and interpretation of university and accrediting body policies. The office interfaces with resident and fellow trainees, program directors, department chairs, and other hospital-wide stakeholders to support our trainees and provide the best clinical learning environment possible.    Required Qualifications Board certified in an appropriate specialty Eligible for appointment to the Ohio State University Wexner Medical Center Medical Staff Academic accomplishments sufficient for appointment at the rank of Associate Professor or Professor Minimum of 5 years of experience in graduate medical education leadership within an academic medical center Extensive knowledge of accreditation standards and GME operations Demonstrated physician executive and administrative leadership skills Strong communication and interpersonal skills Expert knowledge of ACGME Institutional Requirements and Program Requirements Experience leading Graduate Medical Education Committees (GMECs) and institutional governance processes Knowledge of NRMP, ERAS, ECFMG, ABMS, resident credentialing, and trainee appointment processes Experience developing and implementing GME policies, procedures, and affiliation agreements Experience managing budgets, institutional resources, and operational performance metrics Experience integrating resident education with quality improvement, patient safety, and physician well-being initiatives Preferred Qualifications Minimum of 2 years of experience in a significant GME leadership role, such as: Designated Institutional Official (DIO); Associate DIO; GME Committee Chair; Vice Dean for Education Experience overseeing more than 50residency and fellowship programs Experience providing institutional oversight within a large, complex academic health system with multiple clinical training sites Experience leading an institution through successful ACGME accreditation reviews, CLER visits, or institutional self-study processes Demonstrated experience in patient safety, quality improvement, and healthcare outcome initiatives Time Commitment and Appointment The role is structured with approximately 80% effort dedicated to Graduate Medical Education leadership responsibilities, encompassing service as the Designated Institutional Official (DIO) and Associate Medical Director for Graduate Medical Education. The remaining up to 20% effort may be dedicated to clinical practice, which allows for an active clinical presence while prioritizing institutional educational leadership.  One-year renewable appointment based on annual performance evaluation Key Responsibilities Provide institution-wide oversight of all accredited and non-accredited residency and fellowship programs (including dental and podiatry programs) Maintain compliance with ACGME Institutional and Program Requirements and other applicable accrediting bodies Serve as primary institutional contact for accreditation reviews, submissions, correspondence, and major program changes Chair and lead the Graduate Medical Education Committee (GMEC) Oversee program reviews, institutional reviews, continuous quality improvement, and corrective action plans Ensure recruitment, onboarding, credentialing, and support of residents and fellows Promote educational excellence, trainee well-being, professionalism, and faculty development Collaborate with quality and safety leaders to integrate trainees into patient safety and quality improvement initiatives Oversee development and maintenance of GME policies, affiliation agreements, and training agreements Lead the Office of Graduate Medical Education and oversee administrative operations and budget Collaborate with hospital, finance, compliance, and departmental leaders on workforce planning, funding, and strategic initiatives Represent the institution with ACGME, NRMP, AAMC, affiliate hospitals, and other external organizations Support educational alignment across undergraduate, graduate, and continuing medical education Participate in accreditation, regulatory, quality, safety, and operational committees as appropriate How to Apply Application materials must include a cover letter and a Curriculum Vitae. All inquiries and nominations should be directed in confidence to:   Katlyn Spano, Executive Physician and Faculty Recruiter Katlyn.Spano@osumc.edu Faculty Defined Clinical faculty (regardless of rank)  - Persons with titles of assistant professor-clinical, associate professor-clinical, and professor-clinical. These faculty will have earned a Doctor of Medicine (MD), Doctor of Osteopathic Medicine (DO), doctorate (PhD) or other terminal degree in the relevant field. Clinical faculty members are not eligible for tenure and are appointed for terms of three to five years. They may be reappointed to successive terms. Their primary career focus is the delivery of exemplary clinical care, which may also include teaching and/or research. ​ Tenure-Track faculty (regardless of rank)  - Persons with the titles of assistant professor, associate professor, or professor who are eligible for tenure or who have obtained tenure. These faculty will have earned a doctorate (PhD), Doctor of Medicine (MD), Doctor of Osteopathic Medicine (DO), or other terminal degree in the relevant field of study. Their primary career focus centers on research, service, and teaching. Tenure-track faculty with MD, DO, or MD/PhD degrees may also be involved in clinical care.​ Research Faculty (regardless of rank)  - Persons with the titles of research assistant professor, research associate professor, or research professor. These faculty will have earned a doctorate (PhD) or other terminal degree in the relevant field of study. Research faculty members are not eligible for tenure and are appointed for terms of one to five years. Their principal career focus is research.​ Graduate Medical Education at The Ohio State University The Ohio State University Hospitals is the official sponsoring institution for all ACGME-accredited training programs at Ohio State. The GME Office oversees a total of 176 GME training programs with a total of 1,071 trainees.  Of those, there are 24 ACGME-accredited residencies with 752 residents and 58 ACGME-accredited fellowships with 241 fellows.  There are 94 programs that are either non-accredited or accredited by other organizations (e.g., CPME, CODA) with a total of 78 trainees this past year.  In addition to these programs, the College of Medicine’s Department of Pediatrics oversees an additional set of pediatric-focused GME programs at Nationwide Children’s Hospital (NCH), which is a separate Sponsoring Institution for GME purposes.  Given the significant overlap between institutions, the DIO at NCH is a key collaborator for this role, but this role does not have any official responsibilities within the NCH Sponsoring Institution. NCH serves as our most robust GME affiliate institution with a number of OSU GME programs sending residents and fellows to NCH for pediatric components of their training. There is one centralized GME Office with 9 total staff members that interfaces with program coordinators across individual clinical departments and divisions.  This role oversees the GME Office in collaboration with the Director of GME who runs the office day-to-day.  The Ohio State University College of Medicine The Ohio State University College of Medicine, with more than 5,000 learners, is the only academic medical center in central Ohio. Recognized as a Tier 1 school for research in the 2026 U.S. News & World Report “Best Medical Schools” list, it ranks among the top medical schools for research in the nation. It is also recognized as Tier 3 for primary care, further demonstrating its strength across the academic spectrum. Known for its innovative curriculum, cutting-edge research, and excellence in clinical care through The Ohio State University Wexner Medical Center, the college continues to advance its mission of education, discovery, and community impact. With more than 2,700 faculty across 20 clinical departments, eight basic science departments, and the School of Health and Rehabilitation Sciences, Ohio State is shaping the future of academic medicine - locally, nationally, and globally. The Wexner Medical Center The Ohio State University Wexner Medical Center comprises The Ohio State University College of Medicine and its School of Health and Rehabilitation Sciences; various research centers, programs and institutes; six hospitals; and more than 100 ambulatory and multispecialty facilities. The Ohio State Wexner Medical Center is dedicated to providing world-class care for every person, every time; to delivering relentless innovation to improve lives; and to making this the best place to work and learn. We’re proud to celebrate our 34th consecutive year ranked on the U.S. News & World Report “Best Hospitals” list. Best Hospitals rankings are based on outcomes, structure, patient experience and expert opinion. This year, the Wexner Medical Center has five nationally ranked specialties out of 14 – Cancer; Neurology and Neurosurgery; Pulmonology and Lung Surgery; Rehabilitation; and Rheumatology – and two selected as high performing – Gastroenterology and GI Surgery and Geriatrics. U.S. News consistently ranks the Wexner Medical Center as the No. 1 adult hospital in Columbus. Our new University Hospital, which opened in February 2026, is the largest single-facility project ever undertaken at Ohio State. It’s designed to enhance a unified Wexner Medical Center campus providing world-class patient care, leading-edge research and outstanding clinical training. It features: 820 large, private rooms to elevate patient-centered care, nearly doubling the number of beds we have today26 floors, comprising 1.9 million square feet of innovative treatment space Enhanced connections between the new University Hospital and the OSUCCC – James More than half an acre of terrace space and a quarter acre of green space for a small park148 additional beds for patients of the OSUCCC – James 51 neonatal intensive care unit bassinets State-of-the-art diagnostic, treatment and inpatient service areas; imaging; operating rooms; and critical care and medical/surgical beds The University Ohio State is a top-20 public university, and its Ohio State Wexner Medical Center is one of America’s leading academic health centers. Eligible Ohio State employees receive comprehensive benefits packages, including medical, dental, and vision insurance, tuition assistance for employees and their dependents, and state or alternative retirement options with competitive employer contributions. Ohio State is a dynamic community where opportunity thrives, and individuals transform themselves and their world. Positions are available in countless fields and specialties. Become a Buckeye and contribute to an incredible legacy that serves to guide our future and shape a better tomorrow. Columbus, Ohio Located in Ohio’s capital city, Ohio State’s Columbus campus is near the center of a rapidly growing and diverse metropolitan area with a population of over 1.5 million. The area offers a wide range of affordable housing, many cultural and recreational opportunities, excellent schools, and a strong economy based on government as well as service, transportation, and technology industries. Additional information about the Columbus area is available here . In addition to  its Columbus campus, Ohio State has four regional campuses including Ohio State Lima, Ohio State Mansfield, Ohio State Marion, and Ohio State Newark, in addition to Ohio State ATI in Wooster.  The university is an equal opportunity employer, including veterans and disability. Additional Information: Location: Twelfth Ave, 395 W (0356) Position Type: Regular Scheduled Hours: 40 Shift: First Shift The Ohio State University believes in diversity in people and ideas. What Ohio State does matters. And how we do it matters. When we are at our best, we make a real difference to people. Our size, breadth, geography, history and standard of excellence position us to drive the future of higher education. We believe that the university should be a place where people can work and learn together in a safe environment, free of violence, harassment, discrimination, exploitation, or intimidation. As such, finalists for any faculty position that carries tenure must sign an authorization and disclosure form, which will allow current or prior employer(s) to share information to the University regarding any findings of employment-related misconduct or disciplinary proceedings against a candidate and/or any pending investigations related to alleged misconduct. Such information shall include findings and pending investigations with respect to sexual harassment, violence, or harassment; research misconduct; financial fraud or misconduct; foreign influence violations, grant misuse or misconduct; and/or any other type of finding or pending investigation relating to a candidate’s employer’s policies and rules governing faculty conduct that may reasonably be expected to affect a candidate’s appointment. If the University becomes aware of any past finding of misconduct or pending investigations that were not disclosed, Ohio State will treat that as a serious omission and reserves the right to rescind an offer or terminate employment. Final candidates are subject to successful completion of a background check.  A drug screen or physical may be required during the post offer process. Thank you for your interest in positions at The Ohio State University and Wexner Medical Center. Once you have applied, the most updated information on the status of your application can be found by visiting the Candidate Home section of this site. Please view your submitted applications by logging in and reviewing your status. For answers to additional questions please review the frequently asked questions . The university is an equal opportunity employer, including veterans and disability."
    },
    "https://osu.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/OSUCareers/job/Columbus-Campus/Post-Doctoral-Scholar_R160623": {
      "title": "Post Doctoral Scholar",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-16\nFull time\n\nScreen reader users may encounter difficulty with this site. For assistance with applying, please contact hr-accessibleapplication@osu.edu . If you have questions while submitting an application, please review these frequently asked questions . Current Employees and Students: If you are currently employed or enrolled as a student at The Ohio State University, please l og in to Workday to use the internal application process. Welcome to The Ohio State University's career site.  We invite you to apply to positions of interest. In order to ensure your application is complete, you must complete the following: Ensure you have all necessary documents available when starting the application process. You can review the additional job description section on postings for documents that may be required. Prior to submitting your application, please review and update (if necessary) the information in your candidate profile as it will transfer to your application.  Job Title: Post Doctoral Scholar Department: EHE | Human Sciences Administration Position Overview The Health and Nutritional Metabolomics Laboratory at The Ohio State University is seeking a highly motivated Postdoctoral Scholar for an American Heart Association (AHA)-supported research program focused on developing and applying advanced metabolomics and lipidomics technologies to human clinical trial research. The postdoctoral scholar will be jointly mentored by Dr. Chris Zhu and Dr. Colleen Spees , providing a unique opportunity to gain expertise in translational metabolomics, nutrition science, and clinical research. The successful candidate will work within a multidisciplinary team investigating the metabolic mechanisms underlying cardiometabolic diseases and other chronic health conditions through comprehensive analyses of samples collected from human intervention and clinical studies. The Zhu Laboratory utilizes targeted and untargeted metabolomics, lipidomics, mass spectrometry, systems biology, and bioinformatics approaches to identify novel biomarkers and therapeutic targets. The Spees Laboratory has extensive expertise in clinical nutrition, dietary interventions, and patient-centered translational research. Initial appointment: Two years (with an expected starting date of 4/1/2027 ) Renewal: Possible based on funding availability and satisfactory performance Location: Columbus, Ohio Additional Information: Research Opportunities The successful candidate will participate in cutting-edge research involving: Targeted and untargeted metabolomics analyses of human clinical trial samples Comprehensive lipidomics profiling for biomarker discovery Integration of metabolomics and lipidomics data with clinical, dietary, and physiological outcomes Identification of metabolic pathways associated with cardiovascular, metabolic, and chronic diseases Development and application of advanced LC-MS-based analytical methods Multi-omics data integration and systems biology approaches Translation of omics discoveries into clinically relevant applications   Required Qualifications Ph.D. in Analytical Chemistry, Chemistry, Biochemistry, Nutrition, Biomedical Sciences, Life Sciences, or a related discipline Strong background in mass spectrometry-based metabolomics and/or lipidomics Experience with LC-MS analyses and quantitative bioanalytical methods Demonstrated publication record in peer-reviewed journals Excellent analytical, problem-solving, and communication skills Ability to work independently while contributing effectively within a collaborative team environment   Preferred Qualifications Experience working with human clinical trial samples Experience in targeted and untargeted metabolomics workflows Experience in lipidomics analyses and interpretation Knowledge of bioinformatics and statistical tools, including R, MetaboAnalyst, Python, Cytoscape, or related software Experience integrating omics data with clinical or dietary outcomes Research experience in cardiovascular disease, metabolic disease, obesity, diabetes, nutrition, inflammation, or related fields Familiarity with systems biology and biomarker discovery approaches Application Instructions Interested candidates should submit the following materials as a single PDF file : Required Documents Curriculum Vitae (CV) including: Educational background Complete publication list Names and contact information for at least three professional references Research Statement (1–2 pages) describing: Previous and current research experience Research accomplishments and interests Future career goals   Location: Campbell Hall (0018) Position Type: Term (Fixed Term) Scheduled Hours: 40 Shift: First Shift Final candidates are subject to successful completion of a background check.  A drug screen or physical may be required during the post offer process. Thank you for your interest in positions at The Ohio State University and Wexner Medical Center. Once you have applied, the most updated information on the status of your application can be found by visiting the Candidate Home section of this site. Please view your submitted applications by logging in and reviewing your status. For answers to additional questions please review the frequently asked questions . The university is an equal opportunity employer, including veterans and disability."
    },
    "https://osu.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/OSUCareers/job/Columbus-Campus/Assistant-Professor---Empire-and-Environment-in-Latin-America_R160266": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor - Empire and Environment in Latin America",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-16\nFull time\n\nScreen reader users may encounter difficulty with this site. For assistance with applying, please contact hr-accessibleapplication@osu.edu . If you have questions while submitting an application, please review these frequently asked questions . Current Employees and Students: If you are currently employed or enrolled as a student at The Ohio State University, please l og in to Workday to use the internal application process. Welcome to The Ohio State University's career site.  We invite you to apply to positions of interest. In order to ensure your application is complete, you must complete the following: Ensure you have all necessary documents available when starting the application process. You can review the additional job description section on postings for documents that may be required. You will be presented with the opportunity to attach up to five (5) documents in the Application Documents section. If necessary, please consider combining documents into one file. If you require any assistance with including the necessary documents in the application process, please reach out to HRConnection@osu.edu . Resume parsing is not effective on documents which exceed one (1) page.  As a result, it is not recommended to select Autofill with Resume when applying if using a resume or CV which exceeds one (1) page. Prior to submitting your application, please review and update (if necessary) the information in your candidate profile as it will transfer to your application.  Job Title: Assistant Professor - Empire and Environment in Latin America Department: Arts and Sciences | Spanish and Portuguese Transnational World Language Studies Empire and Environment in the Spanish and Portuguese-Speaking Worlds Rank: Tenure-Track Assistant Professor Department of Spanish and Portuguese College of Arts and Sciences   Position Overview The Department of Spanish and Portuguese (SPPO) at The Ohio State University invites applications for a Tenure-Track Assistant Professor position in Transnational World Language Studies in the area of Empire and Environment in the Spanish and/or Portuguese-Speaking areas of Latin America and the Caribbean beginning August 15, 2027. We aim to hire a dynamic scholar who will contribute to the vibrant and collegial intellectual communities of our department by engaging with interdisciplinary research, innovative teaching, program development, community engagement, and service.   This is one of three tenure-track hires in Transnational World Language Studies, the other two being in the departments of East Asian Languages and Literatures and Slavic and East Europeans Languages and Cultures. We are also conducting searches for three Postdoctoral Scholar to Tenure-Track Assistant Professor positions in Humanities-based Interdisciplinary World Languages and Cultures in the departments of French and Italian, Germanic Languages and Literatures, and Near Eastern and South Asian Languages and Cultures .   In keeping with the department’s commitment to interdisciplinarity, we are especially interested in scholars who demonstrate an interdisciplinary research profile that examines the historical and contemporary relationships among empire, environment, extraction, race, indigeneity, migration, and ecological justice across the Spanish- and Portuguese-speaking world. We seek scholars whose research approaches environmental questions through the lenses of, for example, cultural studies, literary studies, Indigenous studies, Latinx studies, decolonial studies, critical geography, environmental humanities, media studies, visual culture, or public humanities. Areas of specialization may include colonial ecologies; extractivism and resource frontiers; environmental injustices; Indigenous and Black Atlantic environmental knowledge; climate migration; food systems; water politics; energy humanities; environmental media; or the historic and cultural dimensions of planetary crisis.   The successful candidate will build on SPPO's existing strengths in Latin American, Latinx, Brazilian, Andean, Iberian, and community-engaged scholarship and will contribute to strengthening connections across world languages while helping to cultivate a more unified intellectual identity around themes of global relevance.   Partial startup for the Transnational World Language Studies tenure-track positions is funded by the Mellon Foundation grant “Global Fluency, Career Agility: Transforming World Languages at Ohio State.” The new hires will participate in the activities within the Department, create new courses, and have designated faculty mentors who are engaged in research on topics relating to transnational world language education, global fluency and intercultural competence, and their area of specialization.   Performance Objectives Develop and maintain an active, nationally and internationally recognized research agenda on empire, environment, and transnational cultural dynamics in the Spanish- and/or Portuguese-speaking world. Teach and mentor undergraduate and graduate students in Spanish and/or Portuguese while contributing to curriculum development in areas such as environmental humanities, climate justice, coloniality, ecology, and transnational cultural studies. Advance the Mellon-funded Transforming World Languages at OSU initiative by collaborating with faculty and staff across disciplines, contributing to innovative programming, and creating new courses that strengthen students’ global fluency and intercultural competence. Foster connections across languages, disciplines, and communities through collaborative research, public-facing scholarship, and/or community-engaged activities. Participate in departmental service.   Education and Experience Qualifications Required: PhD in Spanish, Portuguese, Latin American Cultural Studies, or related field required at time of appointment which is August 15, 2027. ABDs welcome to apply. Native or near-native fluency in Spanish and/or Portuguese Candidates must demonstrate achievement or strong promise of scholarly success. Research agenda that connects multiple methods and/or theoretical approaches to the study of empire and environment in the Americas.   Desired: Experience and/or commitment to public-facing or community engaged scholarship, teaching and/or service. Demonstrated ability to contribute to and strengthen transdisciplinary and translingual collaborations in environmental humanities scholarship. Knowledge of French, Haitian Kreyòl/Creole, and/or Francophone Caribbean contexts, particularly in dialogue with Spanish- and Portuguese-speaking worlds .   Ohio State provides access to a depth and breadth of opportunities and resources. Starting your first day, Ohio State offers you a comprehensive benefits package .   How to Apply To be considered, please submit your application via Workday. https://osu.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/OSUCareers/job/Columbus-Campus/Assistant-Professor---Empire-and-Environment-in-Latin-America_R160266 Application period closes November 9, 2026. Inquiries may be directed to Laura Podalsky, podalsky.1@osu.edu.   You will be presented with the opportunity to attach up to FIVE documents in the Application Documents section. Please include the following: Attachment 1: Cover Letter : 1-2 page letter, which should include a brief summary of your academic background and why you are interested in this opportunity. Attachment 2: CV (Curriculum Vitae) : Detailed overview of your scholarly experience, including your research experience, teaching and mentoring experience, service, engagement, and publications. Attachment 3: Research Statement: Summary of your past research accomplishments, current work/research, and proposal for your future research plan as a faculty member. Attachment 4: Teaching Statement : A statement of your approaches, experience and philosophy regarding your teaching, learning, and mentoring. Attachment 5: A writing sample.   Candidates selected to move forward in the process will be asked to provide three letters of reference. Application period closes November 9, 2026 Additional Information: The College With more than 80 majors and 100 minors, the College of Arts and Sciences is the academic heart of the university. The Arts and Sciences provides extraordinary opportunities to collaborate across disciplines, blending creativity and analysis to truly be at the forefront of thought. The breadth and depth of knowledge in the college gives students and researchers the critical thinking and adaptability essential for a lifetime of success.   Department Information The mission of the Department of Spanish and Portuguese is to teach, create and exchange knowledge about the languages, linguistics, literatures and cultures of Latin America, Portugal, Spain, the United States, and Portuguese- and Spanish-speaking Africa and Asia through teaching and learning, research and creative activity, and community engagement from local to global levels. The Department of Spanish and Portuguese values collaborative scholarship, inclusive excellence, innovative teaching, and community-engaged learning. We seek to a welcoming academic community that advances multilingualism, intercultural understanding, and student success.   The University Ohio State is a top-20 public university, and its Ohio State Wexner Medical Center is one of America’s leading academic health centers. Eligible Ohio State employees receive comprehensive benefits packages, including medical, dental and vision insurance, tuition assistance for employees and their dependents, and state or alternative retirement options with competitive employer contributions.   Grounded in Ohio State University’s Shared Values , our university community welcomes differences, encourages open-minded exploration and courageous thinking, and upholds freedom of expression. Ohio State promotes a culture of work-life balance through institutionalized policies, programs, and resources that help faculty, staff, and their families thrive both professionally and personally.   Ohio State is a dynamic community where opportunity thrives, and individuals transform themselves and their world. Positions are available in countless fields and specialties. Become a Buckeye and contribute to an incredible legacy that serves to guide our future and shape a better tomorrow.   Located in Ohio’s capital city, Ohio State’s Columbus campus is near the center of a rapidly growing and vibrant metropolitan area with a population of over 1.5 million. The area offers a wide range of affordable housing, many cultural and recreational opportunities, excellent schools, and a strong economy based on government as well as service, transportation, and technology industries. Additional information about the Columbus area is available here . Beyond its Columbus campus, Ohio State has four regional campuses including Ohio State Lima, Ohio State Mansfield, Ohio State Marion, and Ohio State Newark, in addition to the College of Food, Agricultural, and Environmental Sciences (CFAES) Wooster Campus, which houses Ohio State ATI.   The university is an equal opportunity employer, including veterans and disability.   Final candidates are subject to successful completion of a background check. Additionally, there may be further requirements specific to the college or unit, which could include drug and health screenings, as well as faculty misconduct checks depending on the rank of the position. Location: Hagerty Hall (0037) Position Type: Regular Scheduled Hours: 40 Shift: First Shift The Ohio State University believes in diversity in people and ideas. What Ohio State does matters. And how we do it matters. When we are at our best, we make a real difference to people. Our size, breadth, geography, history and standard of excellence position us to drive the future of higher education. We believe that the university should be a place where people can work and learn together in a safe environment, free of violence, harassment, discrimination, exploitation, or intimidation. As such, finalists for any faculty position that carries tenure must sign an authorization and disclosure form, which will allow current or prior employer(s) to share information to the University regarding any findings of employment-related misconduct or disciplinary proceedings against a candidate and/or any pending investigations related to alleged misconduct. Such information shall include findings and pending investigations with respect to sexual harassment, violence, or harassment; research misconduct; financial fraud or misconduct; foreign influence violations, grant misuse or misconduct; and/or any other type of finding or pending investigation relating to a candidate’s employer’s policies and rules governing faculty conduct that may reasonably be expected to affect a candidate’s appointment. If the University becomes aware of any past finding of misconduct or pending investigations that were not disclosed, Ohio State will treat that as a serious omission and reserves the right to rescind an offer or terminate employment. Final candidates are subject to successful completion of a background check.  A drug screen or physical may be required during the post offer process. Thank you for your interest in positions at The Ohio State University and Wexner Medical Center. Once you have applied, the most updated information on the status of your application can be found by visiting the Candidate Home section of this site. Please view your submitted applications by logging in and reviewing your status. For answers to additional questions please review the frequently asked questions . The university is an equal opportunity employer, including veterans and disability."
    },
    "https://osu.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/OSUCareers/job/Columbus-Campus/College-of-Nursing-Tenured-Tenure-Track-Faculty_R160305-1": {
      "title": "College of Nursing Tenured/Tenure-Track Faculty",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-16\nFull time\n\nScreen reader users may encounter difficulty with this site. For assistance with applying, please contact hr-accessibleapplication@osu.edu . If you have questions while submitting an application, please review these frequently asked questions . Current Employees and Students: If you are currently employed or enrolled as a student at The Ohio State University, please l og in to Workday to use the internal application process. Welcome to The Ohio State University's career site.  We invite you to apply to positions of interest. In order to ensure your application is complete, you must complete the following: Ensure you have all necessary documents available when starting the application process. You can review the additional job description section on postings for documents that may be required. You will be presented with the opportunity to attach up to five (5) documents in the Application Documents section. If necessary, please consider combining documents into one file. If you require any assistance with including the necessary documents in the application process, please reach out to HRConnection@osu.edu . Resume parsing is not effective on documents which exceed one (1) page.  As a result, it is not recommended to select Autofill with Resume when applying if using a resume or CV which exceeds one (1) page. Prior to submitting your application, please review and update (if necessary) the information in your candidate profile as it will transfer to your application.  Job Title: College of Nursing Tenured/Tenure-Track Faculty Department: Nursing | Division of Nursing and Health Sciences Tenure faculty (regardless of rank) - persons with the titles of professor, associate professor, assistant professor and instructor who serve on appointments totaling 50% or more service to the university and who are eligible for tenure or who have obtained tenure. Duties and responsibilities are assigned in accordance with the workload guidelines laid out in the pattern of administration of each faculty member’s tenure initiation unit (TIU) and, as appropriate, regional campus; obligations will include research, service and/or teaching or clinical practice.  These faculty will have earned doctorate or other terminal degree in the relevant field of study or possession of equivalent experience. Position Overview The Division of Nursing and Health Science invites applications for open-rank, tenured or tenure-track faculty positions at the assistant, associate, or professor level. The Ohio State University is a model 21st century public, land grant, research, urban, community-engaged institution. The College of Nursing is a leading voice and advocate in education, scientific discovery and practice to change the future of healthcare for the better. The Division of Nursing and Health Science serves as a home to nurse and health scientists leading rigorous programs of research addressing critical health challenges across the lifespan, from maternal-infant health to complex care and aging, and many areas in between. This excellence is reflected in our college’s ranking as #6 nationally and #1 among Big Ten nursing schools in NIH funding. We are seeking two faculty members with programs of research well aligned with the visions and missions of our university, college, and division. While all are welcome to apply, we have particular interest in candidates whose work focuses on health informatics/health services research and/or artificial intelligence. Areas of interest include, but are not limited to, clinical and biomedical informatics; digital health infrastructure and health information exchange; health systems research, healthcare delivery, operations, and patient safety; learning health systems and academic-medical center partnerships; clinical decision support; predictive analytics; and the development and responsible use of artificial intelligence, machine learning, and natural language processing to improve health and healthcare. The college is also recognized for its strengths in translational biosciences and dissemination and implementation science. Through our state-of-the-art Translational Biosciences Laboratory and robust interdisciplinary research infrastructure, we welcome candidates whose work advances understanding of, for example, the biological basis of health and disease through biomarker research, biotechnology, stress and other biobehavioral sciences, omics research, systems biology, and precision health. The Helene Fuld Health Trust National Institute for Evidence-based Practice in Nursing and Healthcare provides extensive scholarly resources, mentorship, and methodological expertise to support dissemination and implementation research. We seek scholars who wish to leverage this infrastructure to advance the uptake, sustainability, and scale-up of evidence-based practices across healthcare and community settings. This is not a remote position. Faculty are expected to reside in Ohio and be physically present on campus in Columbus at least three days per week. Performance Objectives Show potential for, demonstrated, or sustained contributions to nursing and/or health science. Develop or expand a nationally or internationally recognized program of research. Show potential for, demonstrated, or sustained internal and external research funding. Contribute to undergraduate and graduate nursing education. Supervise student research and foster critical thinking and inquiry among learners. Demonstrate leadership and collaboration within academic and clinical environments. Serve as an engaged member of the College and broader University community. Show potential for, demonstrated, or sustained evidence of excellence in research mentorship. Exhibit strong interpersonal and communication skills. Demonstrate the ability to work constructively, decisively, and collegially. Demonstrate the ability to work across interdisciplinary teams and with partners. Operate independently as a proactive self-starter. Exhibit effective project leadership and organizational skills. Display innovative thinking, openness, and a commitment to lifelong learning and growth. Participate actively in service to the College of Nursing, university, and professional community. Education and Experience Requirements Required: PhD in Nursing or other related field RN licensure in the State of Ohio for those who are nurses (required upon hire) Desired: Postdoctoral research experience is strongly preferred for those applying at the assistant level Ideal applicants will have research programs focused on health informatics/health services research, artificial intelligence, translational biosciences, and/or dissemination and implementation science, which may be applied to the study of health across the lifespan Additional Information The offer for this position will be based on internal equity and the candidate's qualifications. Sponsorship is not available for international candidates. Ohio State provides access to a depth and breadth of opportunities and resources. Starting your first day, Ohio State offers you a comprehensive benefits package. Application materials must include : Cover letter Curriculum Vita (CV) Career Synopsis (a personal statement highlighting your research, teaching, mentoring, and service. Maximum length 3 pages, single spaced, 1” margins, using Arial 11 pt. font). Applications will be accepted through Monday, November 30, 2026. Additional Information: The College The Ohio State University College of Nursing ranks #6 nationally and #1 among Big Ten nursing schools in NIH funding. As part of one of the nation's leading public research universities and academic health centers, faculty benefit from extensive opportunities for interdisciplinary collaboration, access to diverse clinical and community-based research settings, and a strong culture of research excellence, innovation, and impact. Our colleges’ Division of Nursing and Health Science is home to a diverse community of nurse scientists and health researchers advancing discovery across the lifespan. Through rigorous science, interdisciplinary collaboration, and strong partnerships with patients, families, clinicians, health systems, and communities, our faculty generate evidence that improves health and healthcare and lead innovative research programs spanning clinical, behavioral, implementation, population, and health services research. We are committed to translating discoveries into practice, policy, and measurable health impact. Division faculty also have access to our in-house Translational Biosciences Laboratory, a dedicated research resource that supports a comprehensive range of bio-based research. As an Ohio State-certified Biosafety Level 2 facility, the laboratory provides the infrastructure for rigorous investigation of the biological basis for health and disease, from biospecimen collection, processing, and storage to molecular and cellular investigations, immune and microbial profiling, biomarker discovery and validation, and quantitative analyses of genetic, hormonal, and protein-based measures. The laboratory supports both investigator-initiated and collaborative research across the translational spectrum, enabling faculty to integrate biological measures into clinical, behavioral, population, and health services research programs. Division faculty also have access to the Helene Fuld Health Trust National Institute for Evidence-based Practice in Nursing and Healthcare, an internationally recognized center dedicated to advancing the translation of evidence into practice and improving healthcare outcomes. The Institute provides a rich environment for dissemination and implementation research. Through its extensive training programs, methodological expertise, and national and international collaborations, the Institute supports both investigator-initiated and interdisciplinary research aimed at accelerating the uptake of evidence-based interventions across clinical, community, health system, and community settings. Lastly, division faculty benefit from a rich ecosystem of research-practice partnerships that includes the college's nurse-led Federally Qualified Health Center (FQHC), the Ohio State Wexner Medical Center, the OSUCCC–James Comprehensive Cancer Center, and Nationwide Children's Hospital. Together, these organizations provide exceptional opportunities for clinical, translational, and health systems research across broadly representative populations and settings. Our faculty collaborate with clinicians, scientists, and healthcare leaders to address pressing health challenges, access robust clinical and population health data resources, and translate discoveries into practice. The breadth of these partnerships supports the full continuum of research, from prevention and intervention development to implementation, evaluation, and healthcare transformation, creating unique opportunities to improve health and healthcare delivery. The University Ohio State is a top-20 public university, and its Ohio State Wexner Medical Center is one of America’s leading academic health centers. Eligible Ohio State employees receive comprehensive benefits packages, including medical, dental and vision insurance, tuition assistance for employees and their dependents, and state or alternative retirement options with competitive employer contributions. Grounded in Ohio State University’s Shared Values , our university community welcomes differences, encourages open-minded exploration and courageous thinking, and upholds freedom of expression. Ohio State is a dynamic community where opportunity thrives, and individuals transform themselves and their world. Positions are available in countless fields and specialties. Become a Buckeye and contribute to an incredible legacy that serves to guide our future and shape a better tomorrow. The Ohio State University is committed to enhancing academic excellence. Recruiting, supporting, and retaining faculty of the highest caliber is a core component of this commitment. The Office of Academic Affairs (OAA) has established central resources to focus on offering support to new and prospective faculty and their loved ones. Service offerings include dual career partner consultation, potential employer and/or employment opportunity identification, consultation and resources related to relocation, as well as identifying opportunities to engage on campus and in the surrounding community. While employment opportunities are not guaranteed, resources and consultation are available. More information about dual career and faculty recruitment can be found here . In addition to being responsive to dual-career opportunities, we strongly promote work-life balance to support our community members through a suite of institutionalized policies. Ohio State is a member of the Michigan, Ohio, Western Pennsylvania & West Virginia HERC. Located in Ohio’s capital city, Ohio State’s Columbus campus is near the center of a rapidly growing and vibrant metropolitan area with a population of over 1.5 million. The area offers a wide range of affordable housing, many cultural and recreational opportunities, excellent schools, and a strong economy based on government as well as service, transportation, and technology industries. Additional information about the Columbus area is available here . Beyond its Columbus campus, Ohio State has four regional campuses including Ohio State Lima, Ohio State Mansfield, Ohio State Marion, and Ohio State Newark, in addition to the College of Food, Agricultural, and Environmental Sciences (CFAES) Wooster Campus, which houses Ohio State ATI. The university is an equal opportunity employer, including veterans and disability.  Final candidates are subject to successful completion of a background check. Additionally, there may be further requirements specific to the college or unit, which could include drug and health screenings, as well as faculty misconduct checks depending on the rank of the position Location: Heminger Hall (1069) Position Type: Regular Scheduled Hours: 40 Shift: First Shift The Ohio State University believes in diversity in people and ideas. What Ohio State does matters. And how we do it matters. When we are at our best, we make a real difference to people. Our size, breadth, geography, history and standard of excellence position us to drive the future of higher education. We believe that the university should be a place where people can work and learn together in a safe environment, free of violence, harassment, discrimination, exploitation, or intimidation. As such, finalists for any faculty position that carries tenure must sign an authorization and disclosure form, which will allow current or prior employer(s) to share information to the University regarding any findings of employment-related misconduct or disciplinary proceedings against a candidate and/or any pending investigations related to alleged misconduct. Such information shall include findings and pending investigations with respect to sexual harassment, violence, or harassment; research misconduct; financial fraud or misconduct; foreign influence violations, grant misuse or misconduct; and/or any other type of finding or pending investigation relating to a candidate’s employer’s policies and rules governing faculty conduct that may reasonably be expected to affect a candidate’s appointment. If the University becomes aware of any past finding of misconduct or pending investigations that were not disclosed, Ohio State will treat that as a serious omission and reserves the right to rescind an offer or terminate employment. Final candidates are subject to successful completion of a background check.  A drug screen or physical may be required during the post offer process. Thank you for your interest in positions at The Ohio State University and Wexner Medical Center. Once you have applied, the most updated information on the status of your application can be found by visiting the Candidate Home section of this site. Please view your submitted applications by logging in and reviewing your status. For answers to additional questions please review the frequently asked questions . The university is an equal opportunity employer, including veterans and disability."
    },
    "https://osu.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/OSUCareers/job/College-of-MedicineOffice-of-Health-Sciences/Assistant-Professor_R160928-1": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-16\nFull time\n\nScreen reader users may encounter difficulty with this site. For assistance with applying, please contact hr-accessibleapplication@osu.edu . If you have questions while submitting an application, please review these frequently asked questions . Current Employees and Students: If you are currently employed or enrolled as a student at The Ohio State University, please l og in to Workday to use the internal application process. Welcome to The Ohio State University's career site.  We invite you to apply to positions of interest. In order to ensure your application is complete, you must complete the following: Ensure you have all necessary documents available when starting the application process. You can review the additional job description section on postings for documents that may be required. You will be presented with the opportunity to attach up to five (5) documents in the Application Documents section. If necessary, please consider combining documents into one file. If you require any assistance with including the necessary documents in the application process, please reach out to HRConnection@osu.edu . Resume parsing is not effective on documents which exceed one (1) page.  As a result, it is not recommended to select Autofill with Resume when applying if using a resume or CV which exceeds one (1) page. Prior to submitting your application, please review and update (if necessary) the information in your candidate profile as it will transfer to your application.  Job Title: Assistant Professor Department: Medicine | Pediatrics Tenure faculty (regardless of rank) - persons with the titles of professor, associate professor, assistant professor and instructor who serve on appointments totaling 50% or more service to the university and who are eligible for tenure or who have obtained tenure. Duties and responsibilities are assigned in accordance with the workload guidelines laid out in the pattern of administration of each faculty member’s tenure initiation unit (TIU) and, as appropriate, regional campus; obligations will include research, service and/or teaching or clinical practice.  These faculty will have earned doctorate or other terminal degree in the relevant field of study or possession of equivalent experience. Additional Information: Location: Children's Hospital - Education Center (0885) Position Type: Regular Scheduled Hours: 40 Shift: Varying Shifts The Ohio State University believes in diversity in people and ideas. What Ohio State does matters. And how we do it matters. When we are at our best, we make a real difference to people. Our size, breadth, geography, history and standard of excellence position us to drive the future of higher education. We believe that the university should be a place where people can work and learn together in a safe environment, free of violence, harassment, discrimination, exploitation, or intimidation. As such, finalists for any faculty position that carries tenure must sign an authorization and disclosure form, which will allow current or prior employer(s) to share information to the University regarding any findings of employment-related misconduct or disciplinary proceedings against a candidate and/or any pending investigations related to alleged misconduct. Such information shall include findings and pending investigations with respect to sexual harassment, violence, or harassment; research misconduct; financial fraud or misconduct; foreign influence violations, grant misuse or misconduct; and/or any other type of finding or pending investigation relating to a candidate’s employer’s policies and rules governing faculty conduct that may reasonably be expected to affect a candidate’s appointment. If the University becomes aware of any past finding of misconduct or pending investigations that were not disclosed, Ohio State will treat that as a serious omission and reserves the right to rescind an offer or terminate employment. Final candidates are subject to successful completion of a background check.  A drug screen or physical may be required during the post offer process. Thank you for your interest in positions at The Ohio State University and Wexner Medical Center. Once you have applied, the most updated information on the status of your application can be found by visiting the Candidate Home section of this site. Please view your submitted applications by logging in and reviewing your status. For answers to additional questions please review the frequently asked questions . The university is an equal opportunity employer, including veterans and disability."
