    "https://osu.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/OSUCareers/job/Columbus-Campus/Senior-Associate-Vice-President-for-Research-Operations_R159476-2": {
      "title": "Senior Associate Vice President for Research Operations",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-28\nFull time\n\nScreen reader users may encounter difficulty with this site. For assistance with applying, please contact hr-accessibleapplication@osu.edu . If you have questions while submitting an application, please review these frequently asked questions . Current Employees and Students: If you are currently employed or enrolled as a student at The Ohio State University, please l og in to Workday to use the internal application process. Welcome to The Ohio State University's career site.  We invite you to apply to positions of interest. In order to ensure your application is complete, you must complete the following: Ensure you have all necessary documents available when starting the application process. You can review the additional job description section on postings for documents that may be required. You will be presented with the opportunity to attach up to five (5) documents in the Application Documents section. If necessary, please consider combining documents into one file. If you require any assistance with including the necessary documents in the application process, please reach out to HRConnection@osu.edu . Resume parsing is not effective on documents which exceed one (1) page.  As a result, it is not recommended to select Autofill with Resume when applying if using a resume or CV which exceeds one (1) page. Prior to submitting your application, please review and update (if necessary) the information in your candidate profile as it will transfer to your application.  Job Title: Senior Associate Vice President for Research Operations Department: ERIK | Administration Position Overview The Senior Associate Vice President for Research Operations (SAVP) is a senior executive leader within the Enterprise for Research, Innovation and Knowledge (ERIK) and serves as a strategic partner to the Vice President for Research. This position provides leadership, oversight, and direction for Ohio State’s research operations infrastructure, ensuring excellence in research administration, regulatory compliance, research integrity, and operational effectiveness across the university’s research enterprise. The SAVP leads critical research support and compliance functions, including Sponsored Programs, Responsible Research Practices, University Laboratory Animal Resources, the Animal Care and Use Program, the Biosafety Program, and ERIK’s Instructional Design team. Serving as the university’s designated Institutional Official, the SAVP ensures compliance with federal, state, and institutional regulations governing research activities and oversees institutional research assurances, regulatory relationships, and university-wide compliance programs. Working collaboratively with university leadership, the Wexner Medical Center, Comprehensive Cancer Center, Office of Legal Affairs, Office of Academic Affairs, and other campus stakeholders, the SAVP advances operational excellence and develops best-in-class research administration practices that support faculty, staff, and student researchers. The position plays a central role in implementing ERIK’s strategic plan, driving growth in externally sponsored research, reducing administrative burden, and enhancing the effectiveness and efficiency of research support services. The SAVP is accountable for substantially enhancing day-to-day operations across the research operations portfolio, with a focus on improving service quality, strengthening process discipline, increasing consistency across units, reducing unnecessary administrative burden, and ensuring that operational practices are aligned with institutional strategy. This position is expected to translate ERIK and university-level research strategies into actionable operating plans, measurable priorities, implementation roadmaps, and consistent management practices across the functions within the SAVP portfolio. The SAVP serves as a visible ambassador for Ohio State’s research enterprise, representing the university at the state and national levels through professional organizations, boards, councils, and external partnerships. The successful candidate will bring extensive experience in research administration and compliance, a collaborative and consensus-building leadership style, exceptional communication and relationship management skills, and a demonstrated ability to lead complex organizations and engage diverse stakeholders within a large research-intensive university environment. An earned doctorate, terminal degree, or equivalent combination of leadership experience and expertise is require Position Details The Senior Associate Vice President for Research Operations (SAVP) is a member of the core leadership team of the Enterprise for Research, Innovation and Knowledge (ERIK) and reports directly to the Vice President of Research (VPR). This position plays a key role in ensuring Ohio State is a leading university in research and creative expression. This individual must possess the personal, intellectual, and leadership qualities to implement strategy, oversee administration, and enable the growth of Ohio State’s research operations. The SAVP will direct the operations of multiple teams, including Sponsored Programs, Responsible Research Practices, University Lab Animals Resources, Animal Care and Use Program and Biosafety Program. This focus allows the position to address some of the most complex and consequential challenges facing the institution in research. The SAVP will be responsible for converting research enterprise strategies into clear operational priorities, integrated work plans, resource decisions, process improvements, and measurable outcomes. This includes establishing operating rhythms, clarifying ownership across teams, identifying and resolving process bottlenecks, improving cross-functional coordination, and ensuring that the day-to-day work of the research operations portfolio directly advances ERIK’s strategic priorities. At the direction of the vice president for research, the SAVP will serve as the university’s research Institutional Official, ensuring oversight and compliance with all regulatory, ethical, and administrative obligations tied to that role. This individual will manage institutional duties including authorization of research assurances, coordination with federal agencies, and stewardship of university-wide research compliance programs. The SAVP will oversee the PI status process at Ohio State and be expected to work with college associate deans for research (ADRs) to create best-in-class operations to facilitate leading research operation programs at Ohio State. The SAVP will have close working relationships with the Wexner Medical Center, Comprehensive Cancer Center, Office of Legal Affairs, Office of Academic Affairs, and college deans and ADRs. The SAVP will collaborate with other senior leaders within the Enterprise for Research, Innovation and Knowledge (ERIK) and across the university to collectively empower strong linkages for research operations.   The SAVP will lead ERIK’s Instructional Design team, guiding high‑quality learning solutions and ensuring instructional training excellence aligned with ERIK and university goals. The SAVP will be engaged in the implementation of the ERIK strategic plan that leads to operational excellence, significant growth in external research funding, and optimizes services to create highly efficient research and creative expression support that reduces administrative burdens to faculty, staff, and students. The SAVP will establish, monitor, and report on a rigorous set of key performance indicators to demonstrate operational performance, service effectiveness, compliance reliability, process improvement, stakeholder responsiveness, and accountability. The role will be accountable for using these measures to drive transparency, continuous improvement, timely escalation, and disciplined follow-through with ERIK leadership, university partners, and other key stakeholders. The SAVP will represent Research Operations across Ohio and at the national level by serving on boards, councils, and professional associations to advance institutional priorities and influence the research enterprise. Key Accountabilities The SAVP will be accountable for: Translating ERIK and university research strategies into actionable operational plans, implementation milestones, and measurable outcomes. Substantially improving day-to-day operations across the SAVP portfolio through process redesign, service optimization, cross-functional coordination, and continuous improvement. Establishing and managing a rigorous KPI framework that demonstrates performance, progress, risk management, and accountability. Using operational data and stakeholder input to identify barriers, prioritize improvements, and drive timely resolution of issues. Communicating performance, progress, risks, and improvement plans to ERIK leadership, university partners, and other internal and external stakeholders. Building operating practices that support growth in externally sponsored research, reduce administrative burden, and improve the experience of faculty, staff, and student researchers. Required Qualifications It is expected that the SAVP will have exemplary communication skills as well as strong service orientation and interpersonal skills to complement administrative and financial management expertise. The SAVP will have significant knowledge and direct experience in multiple regulatory areas impacting researchers at Ohio State. The SAVP must have a consensus-building leadership style, demonstrated commitment to team building and organization development.  The SAVP must show demonstrated ability to lead/engage/inspire a large, complex research community; must possess experience interacting with a broad range of public and private constituencies; must demonstrate ability to bring a national/international perspective on major research issues; and possess extensive experience in a higher education institution. Possession of an earned doctorate, other appropriate terminal degree or possession of equivalent experience is required.  Desired Qualifications Strong research background, prior leadership role and experience within a large, leading research university. Experience with human subject research and/or serving on IRB committees/operations. Application Materials A CV and cover letter are required to be submitted at the time of application to be considered for this position. Additional Information: Salary The salary for this position is commensurate with experience. Additional Information         Work hours may often exceed forty hours per week and may require non-traditional work hours, including evenings and weekends depending on work projects and volume.  Candidates seeking an academic appointment will be evaluated based on their qualifications, professional experience, and scholarly record, consistent with the criteria outlined in the governance documents of the applicable department or academic unit. Eligibility for an academic appointment will be determined through this review process. Ohio State will not sponsor applicants for a work visa for this position. Starting Day One: Comprehensive Benefits Ohio State benefits give you options to take care of what matters most to you — and opportunities to make adjustments as your life and needs change, including: Medical, dental and vision coverage, with Ohio State paying a significant portion of the cost. Paid time off, including sick and vacation time and 11 holidays. State retirement plan or an alternative retirement plan, both with generous employer contributions. Enterprise for Research, Innovation and Knowledge Join the Enterprise for Research, Innovation and Knowledge (ERIK) at The Ohio State University and become a part of a dynamic, forward-thinking team dedicated to pushing the boundaries of discovery and innovation. ERIK positions Ohio State to expand curiosity-driven research and creative expression, further develop our research community of faculty, staff, and students, and grow the innovation ecosystem to address big challenges and improve communities locally and globally. We are seeking passionate individuals who are committed to making a meaningful impact through collaboration, innovation, and excellence. To learn more about our mission and work, please visit https://erik.osu.edu/. The University Ohio State is a top-20 public university, and its Ohio State Wexner Medical Center is one of America’s leading academic health centers. Eligible Ohio State employees receive comprehensive benefits packages, including medical, dental and vision insurance, tuition assistance for employees and their dependents, and state or alternative retirement options with competitive employer contributions.   Grounded in Ohio State University’s  Shared Values , our university community welcomes differences, encourages open-minded exploration and courageous thinking, and upholds freedom of expression.   Ohio State is a dynamic community where opportunity thrives, and individuals transform themselves and their world. Positions are available in countless fields and specialties. Become a Buckeye and contribute to an incredible legacy that serves to guide our future and shape a better tomorrow. Located in Ohio’s capital city, Ohio State’s Columbus campus is near the center of a rapidly growing and vibrant metropolitan area with a population of over 1.5 million. The area offers a wide range of affordable housing, many cultural and recreational opportunities, excellent schools, and a strong economy based on government as well as service, transportation, and technology industries. Additional information about the Columbus area is available here . Beyond its Columbus campus, Ohio State has four regional campuses including Ohio State Lima, Ohio State Mansfield, Ohio State Marion, and Ohio State Newark, in addition to the College of Food, Agricultural, and Environmental Sciences (CFAES) Wooster Campus, which houses Ohio State ATI. Location: University Square South (1032) Position Type: Regular Scheduled Hours: 40 Shift: First Shift The Ohio State University believes in diversity in people and ideas. What Ohio State does matters. And how we do it matters. When we are at our best, we make a real difference to people. Our size, breadth, geography, history and standard of excellence position us to drive the future of higher education. We believe that the university should be a place where people can work and learn together in a safe environment, free of violence, harassment, discrimination, exploitation, or intimidation. As such, finalists for any faculty position that carries tenure must sign an authorization and disclosure form, which will allow current or prior employer(s) to share information to the University regarding any findings of employment-related misconduct or disciplinary proceedings against a candidate and/or any pending investigations related to alleged misconduct. Such information shall include findings and pending investigations with respect to sexual harassment, violence, or harassment; research misconduct; financial fraud or misconduct; foreign influence violations, grant misuse or misconduct; and/or any other type of finding or pending investigation relating to a candidate’s employer’s policies and rules governing faculty conduct that may reasonably be expected to affect a candidate’s appointment. If the University becomes aware of any past finding of misconduct or pending investigations that were not disclosed, Ohio State will treat that as a serious omission and reserves the right to rescind an offer or terminate employment. Final candidates are subject to successful completion of a background check.  A drug screen or physical may be required during the post offer process. Thank you for your interest in positions at The Ohio State University and Wexner Medical Center. Once you have applied, the most updated information on the status of your application can be found by visiting the Candidate Home section of this site. Please view your submitted applications by logging in and reviewing your status. For answers to additional questions please review the frequently asked questions . The university is an equal opportunity employer, including veterans and disability."
    },
    "https://osu.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/OSUCareers/job/Columbus-Campus/Post-Doctoral-Scholar_R159483": {
      "title": "Post Doctoral Scholar",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-25\nFull time\n\nScreen reader users may encounter difficulty with this site. For assistance with applying, please contact hr-accessibleapplication@osu.edu . If you have questions while submitting an application, please review these frequently asked questions . Current Employees and Students: If you are currently employed or enrolled as a student at The Ohio State University, please l og in to Workday to use the internal application process. Welcome to The Ohio State University's career site.  We invite you to apply to positions of interest. In order to ensure your application is complete, you must complete the following: Ensure you have all necessary documents available when starting the application process. You can review the additional job description section on postings for documents that may be required. Prior to submitting your application, please review and update (if necessary) the information in your candidate profile as it will transfer to your application.  Job Title: Post Doctoral Scholar Department: Veterinary Medicine | Veterinary Preventive Medicine The College of Veterinary Medicine is seeking applicants for a Post Doctoral Fellow position. The Post Doctoral Scholar will support epidemiological research related to food animal species and public health. The Post Doctoral Scholar will be expected to conduct research, outreach, and provide technical assistance to the PIs, students, and trainees as needed.    The main roles for the individual hired for this position will include:     1) collecting and organizing data for research activities related to livestock species (equine, swine, dairy, poultry, beef), which may involve driving to farms and collecting different types of samples for research purposes, including biosecurity assessments and outbreak investigations at agricultural exhibitions and in livestock production settings;     2) collaborating with the PIs Nolting and Arruda in the generation and testing of research hypotheses and data analysis, writing of papers, grants, and reports, and preparation of presentations and lectures;     3) attending, participating, or presenting at laboratory meetings, seminars, graduate and undergraduate lectures, and conferences as needed;     4) performing miscellaneous office duties as assigned by the PIs including, but not limited to, ordering supplies and helping the PIs in the organization and coordination of meetings and projects;     5) maintaining frequent communication with other faculty and collaborators including students, veterinarians, producers, and livestock industry partners;     6) leading outreach efforts by collaborating and connecting across different members of the future Center and Protect OHIO, translating research findings into outreach-appropriate sessions, building active-learning sessions for stakeholders, learning best practices for educating and engaging stakeholders, and communicating across different agricultural commodities.    Work hours will typically occur between normal business hours but may occasionally require early morning and late evening travel, and weekends sporadically.   This position is available for one year with the possibility of extension based on satisfactory performance and availability of funds.  Applications should include:  (1) letter of intent describing research / outreach interests and fit with this position (2) curriculum vitae/resume (3) three references (contact only; no letters needed).   Working Conditions: Sitting/staring at computer screens for long periods of time. Willingness to travel for outreach events in a regular manner- most of these trips are expected to be within the state of Ohio.    The candidate will have two primary supervisors, therefore the ability to switch between projects, work independently, and being able to manage different tasks in the workweek are expected, along with excellent communication skills.  Additional Information: Minimum Qualifications: PhD in epidemiology or a related field  . Desired Qualifications: F amiliarity with programing language (Stata/R/SAS.); experience in veterinary epidemiology, qualitative research approaches, excellent oral/written communication, leadership and interpersonal skills, and demonstrated ability to work in teams.      Candidates   need to have a valid Driver’s License to drive in the US for research projects.   Location: Sisson Hall (0080) Position Type: Term (Fixed Term) Scheduled Hours: 40 Shift: Varying Shifts Final candidates are subject to successful completion of a background check.  A drug screen or physical may be required during the post offer process. Thank you for your interest in positions at The Ohio State University and Wexner Medical Center. Once you have applied, the most updated information on the status of your application can be found by visiting the Candidate Home section of this site. Please view your submitted applications by logging in and reviewing your status. For answers to additional questions please review the frequently asked questions . The university is an equal opportunity employer, including veterans and disability."
    },
    "https://osu.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/OSUCareers/job/Columbus-Campus/Post-Doctoral-Researcher_R159478-1": {
      "title": "Post Doctoral Researcher",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-25\nFull time\n\nScreen reader users may encounter difficulty with this site. For assistance with applying, please contact hr-accessibleapplication@osu.edu . If you have questions while submitting an application, please review these frequently asked questions . Current Employees and Students: If you are currently employed or enrolled as a student at The Ohio State University, please l og in to Workday to use the internal application process. Welcome to The Ohio State University's career site.  We invite you to apply to positions of interest. In order to ensure your application is complete, you must complete the following: Ensure you have all necessary documents available when starting the application process. You can review the additional job description section on postings for documents that may be required. Prior to submitting your application, please review and update (if necessary) the information in your candidate profile as it will transfer to your application.  Job Title: Post Doctoral Researcher Department: Veterinary Medicine | Veterinary Biosciences Dr. Vladi Karniychuk’s laboratory at the Department of Veterinary Biosciences, The Ohio State University (OSU), is seeking a highly motivated and dedicated postdoctoral scholar to conduct research in virology.     The postdoctoral fellow will focus on the pathogenesis   and vaccine development   against important zoonotic, livestock, and human flaviviruses (WNV, JEV, and YFV) . This project involves animal studies, cell culture, molecular biology ,   and virology techniques   at Biosafety Level 2 and 3 laboratory containment.     The successful candidate will be expected to independently conduct research, assist the principal investigator, and contribute to mentoring and training activities in the lab. Strong writing, analytical, and communication skills are essential.     Applicants must demonstrate a proven ability to complete complex research projects during their Ph.D. and possess significant experience in standard laboratory methods.   The Department of Veterinary Biosciences operates in a team environment, providing access to   experts at all levels, including internationally recognized specialists in virology and immunology. The Ohio State University College of Veterinary Medicine is among the top-rated Veterinary Colleges in the US and internationally. It is also the home of the Center for Retrovirus Research (CRR). The candidate will have an opportunity to become a member of the Infectious Disease Institute (IDI) at The Ohio State University. The IDI membership   comprises over 200 faculty members from various disciplines,   representing 10 colleges across the university. IDI brings together a dynamic research community that fosters a collaborative approach to address critical problems in infectious disease and the global environment.     Columbus, Ohio’s capital and one of the largest cities in the US, offers affordable housing, many cultural and recreational opportunities, excellent schools, easy commute, and a strong economy with growing industries in biomedical sciences, finance, insurance, healthcare, retail, e-commerce, and IT.   Additional Information: Minimum Qualifications: Ph.D Working Conditions: Research in virology in BSL2 and BSL3 conditions.   Location: Veterinary Medicine Academic (0136) Position Type: Term (Fixed Term) Scheduled Hours: 40 Shift: First Shift Final candidates are subject to successful completion of a background check.  A drug screen or physical may be required during the post offer process. Thank you for your interest in positions at The Ohio State University and Wexner Medical Center. Once you have applied, the most updated information on the status of your application can be found by visiting the Candidate Home section of this site. Please view your submitted applications by logging in and reviewing your status. For answers to additional questions please review the frequently asked questions . The university is an equal opportunity employer, including veterans and disability."
    },
    "https://osu.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/OSUCareers/job/College-of-MedicineOffice-of-Health-Sciences/Post-Doctoral-Scholar---Comprehensive-Cancer-Center_R159673-1": {
      "title": "Post Doctoral Scholar - Comprehensive Cancer Center",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-25\nFull time\n\nScreen reader users may encounter difficulty with this site. For assistance with applying, please contact hr-accessibleapplication@osu.edu . If you have questions while submitting an application, please review these frequently asked questions . Current Employees and Students: If you are currently employed or enrolled as a student at The Ohio State University, please l og in to Workday to use the internal application process. Welcome to The Ohio State University's career site.  We invite you to apply to positions of interest. In order to ensure your application is complete, you must complete the following: Ensure you have all necessary documents available when starting the application process. You can review the additional job description section on postings for documents that may be required. Prior to submitting your application, please review and update (if necessary) the information in your candidate profile as it will transfer to your application.  Job Title: Post Doctoral Scholar - Comprehensive Cancer Center Department: Medicine | Radiation Oncology The Guo Laboratory at The Ohio State University is seeking a motivated Post-Doctoral Researcher, eager to support research studying the molecular mechanism of tumor cells manipulating glucose, glutamine and lipid metabolism. The successful candidate will leverage a combination of advanced molecular, in vitro , and systems methodologies to advance our understanding of the key molecular targets in cancer metabolism and translate it to the clinic. This position provides the opportunity of strong mentorship and a close, multidisciplinary collaborative research environment. Primary Responsibilities Lead the state-of-the-art research using genetic mouse models for evaluating therapeutic efficacy, histology, and advanced imaging assays for glioblastoma research. Utilize murine and patient derived cell lines for in vitro modeling to examine tumor cell lipid metabolism. Prepare figures, manuscripts, abstracts, and grant submission materials for publication and/or presentation. Present research findings at lab meetings, institutional seminars, and conferences/symposiums. Mentor graduate students and/or junior trainees as appropriate. Required Qualifications Ph.D. in Neuroscience, Cancer Biology, Cell Biology, or in a comparable field or discipline. Demonstrated ability to work independently and collaboratively in a multidisciplinary research setting. Strong written and verbal communication skills. Preferred Experience ​​​​​​Prior research experience in: neuroscience, cell and molecular biology, cancer biology.  Experience in glioblastoma mouse models, signal transduction, metabolism, immunofluorescence/immunohistochemistry, organoid culturing, and genetic mouse model generation/maintenance. Experience in mouse bioluminescence imaging is ideal. Track record of peer-reviewed publications Prior experience and comfort presenting research. Additional Information: Location: Tzagournis Medical Research Facility (0163) Position Type: Term (Fixed Term) Scheduled Hours: 40 Shift: First Shift Final candidates are subject to successful completion of a background check.  A drug screen or physical may be required during the post offer process. Thank you for your interest in positions at The Ohio State University and Wexner Medical Center. Once you have applied, the most updated information on the status of your application can be found by visiting the Candidate Home section of this site. Please view your submitted applications by logging in and reviewing your status. For answers to additional questions please review the frequently asked questions . The university is an equal opportunity employer, including veterans and disability."
    },
    "https://osu.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/OSUCareers/job/College-of-MedicineOffice-of-Health-Sciences/Director--Center-for-Cancer-Prevention--Faculty--Division-of-Cancer-Prevention-and-Control-_R159747-1": {
      "title": "Director, Center for Cancer Prevention- Faculty (Division of Cancer Prevention and Control)",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-25\nFull time\n\nScreen reader users may encounter difficulty with this site. For assistance with applying, please contact hr-accessibleapplication@osu.edu . If you have questions while submitting an application, please review these frequently asked questions . Current Employees and Students: If you are currently employed or enrolled as a student at The Ohio State University, please l og in to Workday to use the internal application process. Welcome to The Ohio State University's career site.  We invite you to apply to positions of interest. In order to ensure your application is complete, you must complete the following: Ensure you have all necessary documents available when starting the application process. You can review the additional job description section on postings for documents that may be required. You will be presented with the opportunity to attach up to five (5) documents in the Application Documents section. If necessary, please consider combining documents into one file. If you require any assistance with including the necessary documents in the application process, please reach out to HRConnection@osu.edu . Resume parsing is not effective on documents which exceed one (1) page.  As a result, it is not recommended to select Autofill with Resume when applying if using a resume or CV which exceeds one (1) page. Prior to submitting your application, please review and update (if necessary) the information in your candidate profile as it will transfer to your application.  Job Title: Director, Center for Cancer Prevention- Faculty (Division of Cancer Prevention and Control) Department: Medicine | IM Cancer Prevention and Control Tenure faculty (regardless of rank) - persons with the titles of professor, associate professor, assistant professor and instructor who serve on appointments totaling 50% or more service to the university and who are eligible for tenure or who have obtained tenure. Duties and responsibilities are assigned in accordance with the workload guidelines laid out in the pattern of administration of each faculty member’s tenure initiation unit (TIU) and, as appropriate, regional campus; obligations will include research, service and/or teaching or clinical practice.  These faculty will have earned doctorate or other terminal degree in the relevant field of study or possession of equivalent experience. Additional Information: Director, Center for Cancer Prevention- Faculty (Division of Cancer Prevention and Control) The Ohio State University Comprehensive Cancer Center – Arthur G. James Cancer Hospital and Richard J. Solove Research Institute is establishing a new Center for Cancer Prevention dedicated to advancing innovative research, education, implementation, and community-based interventions focused on reducing cancer incidence and cancer-related disparities across Ohio and beyond. As one of the nation's premier comprehensive cancer centers, Ohio State has a longstanding commitment to understanding the causes of cancer and developing evidence-based strategies that prevent cancer before it occurs. Through expertise in epidemiology, behavioral science, genomics, implementation science, cancer screening, health policy, environmental health, community engagement, and population health, Ohio State is uniquely positioned to become a national leader in cancer prevention research and practice. The new Center for Cancer Prevention will serve as a multidisciplinary hub that brings together researchers, clinicians, public health professionals, community organizations, government agencies, healthcare systems, and industry partners to accelerate the discovery, translation, implementation, and dissemination of cancer prevention strategies. The Center will focus on the full spectrum of cancer prevention, including: Primary prevention Cancer risk reduction Behavioral and lifestyle interventions Nutrition and physical activity Obesity and metabolic health Environmental and occupational exposures Vaccination and infection-related cancers Genetic and familial cancer risk Precision prevention Risk prediction and stratification Screening and early detection Rural health and underserved populations Health disparities and health equity Community-engaged prevention research Policy and systems-level prevention strategies Dissemination and implementation science The Center will support the mission of reducing the burden of cancer through scientific discovery, innovation, collaboration, and evidence-based public health action while addressing the unique needs of Ohio's urban, rural, and Appalachian populations. The Division of Cancer Prevention and Control invites applications for the inaugural Director of the Center for Cancer Prevention . The Director will be a nationally or internationally recognized leader whose scholarship, funding, and scientific leadership have significantly advanced the field of cancer prevention, cancer control, or population health. The Director will lead the development and implementation of a bold vision for the new Center and establish Ohio State as a national destination for cancer prevention research, training, policy, and community impact. Working collaboratively with institutional leadership, faculty investigators, healthcare leaders, government agencies, public health organizations, industry partners, and community stakeholders, the Director will create a dynamic and innovative research environment dedicated to reducing cancer incidence and improving population health outcomes. The successful candidate will maintain an active and internationally recognized research program while providing leadership for all aspects of Center development, including strategic planning, faculty engagement, scientific programming, community partnerships, training initiatives, resource development, and fundraising. The Director will have four primary responsibilities: Scientific Leadership Develop and lead a comprehensive scientific agenda that advances the understanding, prevention, and early detection of cancer across populations and healthcare settings.   Center Development and Administration Establish the organizational infrastructure, governance, strategic priorities, and operational framework necessary for a nationally prominent research center.   Research Innovation and Growth Foster a highly collaborative scientific environment that supports groundbreaking research, large-scale funding initiatives, and interdisciplinary partnerships.   Community Engagement and Public Health Impact Build strong partnerships with communities, health systems, public health agencies, policymakers, and advocacy organizations to translate research into meaningful reductions in cancer burden. Required Qualifications PhD, MD, DO, DrPH, PharmD, DNP, PsyD, or other terminal degree in a discipline relevant to cancer prevention. Eligible for appointment as a tenured Professor within the College of Medicine. Nationally recognized record of research excellence in cancer prevention, cancer control, epidemiology, behavioral science, public health, implementation science, risk reduction, or a related field. Demonstrated record of sustained extramural funding. Experience leading multidisciplinary scientific initiatives. Outstanding record of peer-reviewed publications and scholarly achievement. Experience mentoring faculty, fellows, postdoctoral scholars, and students. Strong leadership, communication, and relationship-building skills. Desired Qualifications Internationally recognized leader in cancer prevention research. Experience establishing or directing a research center, institute, consortium, program, or large collaborative network. Track record of leading major NIH, NCI, CDC, PCORI, ACS, CPRIT, foundation, or multinational research initiatives. Expertise in one or more areas including: Cancer epidemiology Precision prevention Behavioral intervention science Tobacco control Obesity and metabolism Screening and early detection Vaccination and infection-related cancers Health disparities Environmental health Community-engaged research Implementation science Population health Experience influencing public health policy or healthcare delivery systems. Demonstrated success recruiting and developing high-performing scientific teams. Experience securing philanthropic investments and strategic partnerships. How to Apply To be considered, please submit your application electronically via Workday. All inquiries and nominations should be directed in confidence to: Anna Godwin, Research Faculty Recruitment, Anna.Godwin@osumc.edu Faculty Defined Clinical faculty (regardless of rank)  - Persons with titles of assistant professor-clinical, associate professor-clinical and professor-clinical. These faculty will have earned a Doctor of Medicine (MD), Doctor of Osteopathic Medicine (DO), doctorate (PhD) or other terminal degree in the relevant field. Clinical faculty members are not eligible for tenure and are appointed for terms of three to five years. They may be reappointed to successive terms. Their principal career focus is the delivery of exemplary clinical care, and may include teaching, and/or research. ​ Tenure-Track faculty (regardless of rank)  - Persons with the titles of assistant professor, associate professor, or professor who are eligible for tenure or who have obtained tenure. These faculty will have earned a doctorate (PhD), Doctor of Medicine (MD), Doctor of Osteopathic Medicine (DO) or other terminal degree in the relevant field of study. Their primary career focus centers on research, service, and teaching. Tenure-track faculty with MD, DO or MD/PhD degrees may also be involved in clinical care.​ Research Faculty (regardless of rank)  - Persons with the titles of research assistant professor, research associate professor, or research professor. These faculty will have earned a doctorate (PhD) or other terminal degree in the relevant field of study. Research faculty members are not eligible for tenure and are appointed for terms of one to five years. Their principal career focus is research.​ The Ohio State University College of Medicine The Ohio State University College of Medicine, with more than 5,000 learners, is the only academic medical center in central Ohio. Recognized as a Tier 1 school for research in the 2025–2026 U.S. News & World Report “Best Medical Schools” list, it ranks among the top 16 medical schools for research in the nation. It also remains in Tier 2 for primary care, further demonstrating its strength across the academic spectrum. Known for its innovative curriculum, cutting-edge research, and excellence in clinical care through The Ohio State University Wexner Medical Center, the college continues to advance its mission of education, discovery and community impact. With more than 2,700 faculty across 20 clinical departments, eight basic science departments, and the School of Health and Rehabilitation Sciences, Ohio State is shaping the future of academic medicine - locally, nationally and globally. The Wexner Medical Center The Ohio State University Wexner Medical Center consists of The Ohio State University Health System, the College of Medicine, the Health Sciences Library and the Faculty Group Practice. The Ohio State Wexner Medical Center is committed to providing world-class care. At the Ohio State Wexner Medical Center, we're proud to be celebrating our 33rd consecutive year ranked in the U.S. News & World Report Best Hospitals list. This year, the Ohio State Wexner Medical Center has 9 nationally ranked specialties out of 15 and four selected as high performing. U.S. News & World Report consistently ranks the Ohio State Wexner Medical Center as the top hospital in Columbus. The Wexner Medical Center is rated as high performing in 17 out of 22 common procedures and conditions, including abdominal aortic aneurysm repair, aortic valve surgery, arrhythmia, COPD, colon cancer surgery, diabetes, gynecological cancer surgery, heart attack, heart failure, kidney failure, leukemia, lymphoma and myeloma, lung cancer surgery, pacemaker implantation, pneumonia, prostate cancer, spinal fusion, and transcatheter aortic valve replacement. Our new hospital tower, opened in February, 2026, is the largest single facilities project ever undertaken at Ohio State. It’s designed to enhance a unified Ohio State Wexner Medical Center campus providing world-class patient care, leading-edge research and outstanding clinical training.    • 820 large, private rooms to elevate patient-centered care, nearly doubling the number of beds we have today    • Enhanced connections between the new inpatient hospital and the OSUCCC – James    • More than half an acre of terrace space and a quarter acre of green space for a small park    • 148 additional beds for patients of the OSUCCC – James    • 51 neonatal intensive care unit bassinets    • State-of-the-art diagnostic, treatment and inpatient service areas; imaging; operating rooms; and critical care and medical/surgical beds The University Ohio State is a top-20 public university, and its Ohio State Wexner Medical Center is one of America’s leading academic health centers and recently ranked No. 4 on Forbes ’ list of best U.S. employers for diversity. Eligible Ohio State employees receive comprehensive benefits packages, including medical, dental and vision insurance, tuition assistance for employees and their dependents, and state or alternative retirement options with competitive employer contributions. Ohio State is a dynamic community where opportunity thrives, and individuals transform themselves and their world. Positions are available in countless fields and specialties. Become a Buckeye and contribute to an incredible legacy that serves to guide our future and shape a better tomorrow. The Ohio State University is committed to enhancing academic excellence. Recruiting, supporting, and retaining faculty of the highest caliber is a core component of this commitment. The Office of Academic Affairs (OAA) has established Dual Careers and Faculty Relocation (DCFR) to focus on supporting new and prospective faculty and their loved ones. Service offerings include dual careers partner consultations, identifying potential employers and/or employment opportunities, consultation and resources related to relocation, as well as identifying opportunities to engage on campus and in the surrounding community. While employment opportunities are not guaranteed, resources and consultation are provided to support the partners of new and prospective faculty as they are considering or transitioning to The Ohio State University. In addition to being responsive to dual-career opportunities, we strongly promote work-life balance to support our community members through a suite of institutionalized policies.  Ohio State is an NSF ADVANCE institution. Columbus, Ohio Located in Ohio’s capital city, Ohio State’s Columbus campus is near the center of a rapidly growing and diverse metropolitan area with a population of over 1.5 million. The area offers a wide range of affordable housing, many cultural and recreational opportunities, excellent schools, and a strong economy based on government as well as service, transportation, and technology industries. Additional information about the Columbus area is available here . In addition to  its Columbus campus, Ohio State has four regional campuses including Ohio State Lima, Ohio State Mansfield, Ohio State Marion, and Ohio State Newark, in addition to Ohio State ATI in Wooster.  Location: Olentangy River Rd, 3650 (2520) Position Type: Regular Scheduled Hours: 40 Shift: First Shift The Ohio State University believes in diversity in people and ideas. What Ohio State does matters. And how we do it matters. When we are at our best, we make a real difference to people. Our size, breadth, geography, history and standard of excellence position us to drive the future of higher education. We believe that the university should be a place where people can work and learn together in a safe environment, free of violence, harassment, discrimination, exploitation, or intimidation. As such, finalists for any faculty position that carries tenure must sign an authorization and disclosure form, which will allow current or prior employer(s) to share information to the University regarding any findings of employment-related misconduct or disciplinary proceedings against a candidate and/or any pending investigations related to alleged misconduct. Such information shall include findings and pending investigations with respect to sexual harassment, violence, or harassment; research misconduct; financial fraud or misconduct; foreign influence violations, grant misuse or misconduct; and/or any other type of finding or pending investigation relating to a candidate’s employer’s policies and rules governing faculty conduct that may reasonably be expected to affect a candidate’s appointment. If the University becomes aware of any past finding of misconduct or pending investigations that were not disclosed, Ohio State will treat that as a serious omission and reserves the right to rescind an offer or terminate employment. Final candidates are subject to successful completion of a background check.  A drug screen or physical may be required during the post offer process. Thank you for your interest in positions at The Ohio State University and Wexner Medical Center. Once you have applied, the most updated information on the status of your application can be found by visiting the Candidate Home section of this site. Please view your submitted applications by logging in and reviewing your status. For answers to additional questions please review the frequently asked questions . The university is an equal opportunity employer, including veterans and disability."
    },
    "https://osu.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/OSUCareers/job/College-of-MedicineOffice-of-Health-Sciences/Associate-Professor-Practice_R159867-1": {
